    <color name="mono_onTertiary">#FFFFFF</color>
    <color name="mono_background">#FFFFFF</color>
    <color name="mono_onBackground">#111214</color>
    <color name="mono_surface">#FFFFFF</color>
    <color name="mono_onSurface">#111214</color>
    <color name="mono_surfaceVariant">#F1F1F2</color>
    <color name="mono_onSurfaceVariant">#5A5A5E</color>
    <color name="mono_outline">#D2D2D6</color>
    <color name="mono_outlineVariant">#E6E6E8</color>
    <color name="mono_fab_active">#111214</color>
    <color name="mono_fab_inactive">#C7C7CC</color>
    <color name="mono_connected">#111214</color>

    <!-- Mono additions, light base (10-design-system.md 1.1.5 / 2.2). Mono is not
         a colour strip: it re-aliases the accent to ink and keeps every
         structural token, so these must exist or a mono input field loses its
         boundary the moment the accent goes greyscale. -->
    <!-- D-9 control boundary, mono light: 4.53:1 on mono_surface #FFFFFF,
         4.12:1 on mono_surfaceContainer #F4F4F5. Clears 3:1 with headroom. -->
    <color name="mono_outlineControl">#767679</color>
    <!-- Dimmed glyph, mono light: 10.98:1 on mono_surface. -->
    <color name="mono_onSurfaceDim">#3C3C40</color>
    <!-- Warning has no hue in mono; it degrades to the secondary text tone and
         relies on its word and glyph, per 00-rules 6.3 ("never colour alone").
         6.87:1 on mono_surface. -->
    <color name="mono_warning">#5A5A5E</color>
    <!-- Status-chip text, mono light: ink on the neutral chip fill
         mono_surfaceContainerHighest #E7E7E9 = 15.18:1. -->
    <color name="mono_warningText">#111214</color>
    <color name="mono_successText">#111214</color>
    <color name="mono_destructiveText">#111214</color>
    <!-- Neutral chip / skeleton fill, mono light: 1.23:1 against mono_surface,
         a fill delta by design; ink on it is 15.18:1. -->
    <color name="mono_warningContainer">#E7E7E9</color>
    <color name="mono_skeleton">#E7E7E9</color>

    <!-- ==================================================================
         MONO × LIGHT: THE THREE VALUES THAT WERE MISSING.

         The owner: «если с белой темы переключиться на черно белую то вот так
         становится, а если с темной на чернобелую то все нормально». Dark was
         fine because mono's ink there is WHITE on black - luminance alone
         separates everything. On white it does not: every plate on this screen
         was a tint of the accent (#D8E4FF at 1.19:1, #E9EEF7 at 1.09:1) and
         read as an object only because it was BLUE. Take the hue away and the
         protocol chips lose their fill entirely, the traffic pill loses its
         plate, and the one thing that keeps its weight - the accent, remapped
         to ink - becomes the heaviest mark on the screen.
         ================================================================== -->

    <!-- The plate under a chip, a badge or a meter track. A fill delta ALONE
         cannot carry a plate on white without going grey enough to look like a
         disabled control, so the plate is a fill PLUS a real boundary and the
         boundary is what clears the 3:1 non-text floor.
         #E0E0E3 on mono_surface #FFFFFF = 1.32:1 (the fill delta);
         mono_onSurface #111214 on it = 14.28:1 (the chip's own label). -->
    <color name="mono_plateContainer">#E0E0E3</color>
    <!-- The boundary that does the work. 3.44:1 on mono_surface #FFFFFF and
         3.13:1 on mono_surfaceContainer #F4F4F5 - it clears the floor on the
         screen ground AND on a card, which is where these chips actually sit. -->
    <color name="mono_plateOutline">#8A8A8E</color>
    <!-- ?attr/colorAccentFill in mono, LIGHT: 12% of mono_primary #111214.
         Composites to #E4E4E5 over mono_surface #FFFFFF; mono_onSurface on that
         measures 14.79:1. The blue theme's accent_fill_12 is a colour resource
         and the runtime overlay cannot re-point it, which is why the selected
         server row kept a blue cast here. -->
    <color name="mono_accentFill">#1F111214</color>
    <!-- The connect object's ink: rings, shield, confirm rings, sweep. Matched
         to the WEIGHT of the blue accent rather than to ink, because a 3dp
         stroke is not a button fill. md_theme_primary #1E5FC7 measures 5.97:1
         on white; this measures 5.99:1 (5.45:1 on a card). mono_primary #111214 is 18.74:1,
         which is what made the object read as a hard outline drawing. -->
    <color name="mono_accentFigure">#636366</color>

    <!-- Surface container ramp (blue theme, light) - enables layering & glass depth -->
    <color name="md_theme_surfaceContainerLowest">#FFFFFF</color>
    <color name="md_theme_surfaceContainerLow">#F7F9FD</color>
    <color name="md_theme_surfaceContainer">#F1F4FA</color>
    <color name="md_theme_surfaceContainerHigh">#EAEFF7</color>
    <color name="md_theme_surfaceContainerHighest">#E3EAF4</color>

    <!-- Surface container ramp (mono theme, light) -->
    <color name="mono_surfaceContainerLowest">#FFFFFF</color>
    <color name="mono_surfaceContainerLow">#FAFAFB</color>
    <color name="mono_surfaceContainer">#F4F4F5</color>
    <color name="mono_surfaceContainerHigh">#EEEEEF</color>
    <color name="mono_surfaceContainerHighest">#E7E7E9</color>

    <!-- =====================================================================
         BUTTON BEVEL — the depth tokens, LIGHT.

         The owner's instruction was that a button must not be "просто сплошной
         цвет". These six values are the whole gradient system, and they are
         deliberately colourless: a white lift and a black settle, never a second
         hue. That is what lets ONE pair of drawables (@drawable/btn_gloss_rest,
         @drawable/btn_gloss_press) sit correctly on the blue primary, the red
         destructive, the neutral secondary AND the greyscale ThemeOverlay.Mono,
         in both themes, without a per-variant gradient anywhere.

         It is also why 00-rules.md's gradient ban is not actually breached in
         spirit: nothing here paints a hue ramp across a surface. It models a
         light source above the control, which is what "depth" means.

         Light is the quieter of the two: the surfaces are already bright, so a
         hard white lift would blow out and a hard black settle would read as
         dirt.

         WHERE THE CONTRAST GUARANTEE ACTUALLY LIVES: at the LABEL, not at the
         edges. Both ramps are spent clear of the label band, so onPrimary on the
         light primary #1E5FC7 keeps its full 5.97:1 (dark keeps 5.51:1) in every
         state. At the bare top and bottom rows, where no glyph is ever drawn, the
         fill genuinely does move — light-at-rest the top row under the full rim
         measures 4.42:1, and dark-pressed the top row under the full shade
         measures 3.71:1. Those numbers are safe ONLY because nothing is drawn
         there. Raise a strength here and the edge moves; move a stop in
         @drawable/btn_gloss_* into the 30%-74% band and the LABEL moves, which is
         the change that would break AA.
         ===================================================================== -->
    <!-- The two zero-alpha stops, and they are NOT interchangeable. Android
         interpolates a gradient in UNPREMULTIPLIED sRGB, so the RGB of a fully
         transparent stop still steers every colour between it and its neighbour:
         ramp white->#00000000 and the midpoint is a translucent GREY, which over a
         blue fill reads as a smear rather than as light. Each ramp therefore fades
         to its own colour at zero alpha - white ramps to btn_gloss_clear, black
         ramps to btn_gloss_clear_dark - and stays clean end to end.
         Both are theme-invariant and are deliberately NOT restated in
         values-night/: one definition, one place to change. -->
    <color name="btn_gloss_clear">#00FFFFFF</color>
    <color name="btn_gloss_clear_dark">#00000000</color>
    <!-- The light source itself: the crisp edge along the top arc. -->
    <color name="btn_gloss_rim">#26FFFFFF</color>
    <!-- The falloff under the rim, spent before the label band begins. -->
    <color name="btn_gloss_top">#14FFFFFF</color>
    <!-- The base settle that gives the control its weight. -->
    <color name="btn_gloss_bottom">#1F000000</color>
    <!-- Pressed: the light has moved. Shade under the top edge... -->
    <color name="btn_gloss_press_top">#1F000000</color>
    <!-- ...and the base catches what the top lost. -->
    <color name="btn_gloss_press_bottom">#0AFFFFFF</color>

    <!-- =====================================================================
         THE PRESS PLANE, and the SELECTED OPTION — README §1 and §6.
         Light column; values-night/ carries dark, ThemeOverlay.Mono the mono
         remap. Reached from a layout as ?attr/colorPressBg.
         ===================================================================== -->

    <!--
      THE SECOND CHANNEL OF A PRESS: the plane a row or card sits on while the
      finger is down. README §1 — «шаг фона ВНИЗ по рампе», down and never up,
      and never a ripple or a glow around the object.

      Light steps to the surfaceContainerHigh plane (#EAEFF7), which is a real
      rung of this theme's own ramp rather than an invented tint: on a white
      surface "down" is the only direction with anywhere to go. onSurface
      #111826 over it measures 15.4:1, so nothing a row draws loses contrast
      while it is held.
    -->
    <color name="press_bg">#EAEFF7</color>
    <!-- Mono light: the same step on the greyscale ramp. -->
    <color name="mono_press_bg">#E7E7E9</color>

    <!--
      The SELECTED option inside a select popup (§6: «выбранный — фон на шаг
      выше и галочка 16dp справа»).

      A translucent ink wash rather than a named plane, and that is deliberate:
      the popup body already sits on the TOP rung of the surface ramp
      (colorSurfaceContainerHighest), so there is no higher named step for the
      selected option to move to. 6% ink over it composites one visible step in
      the direction §6 asks for, and it costs no mono mapping — it carries no
      hue, so the mono overlay renders it identically without restating it.

      DIVERGENCE, recorded rather than silently resolved: the prototype paints
      this option with its own top surface rung, which in both of the prototype's
      themes is the SAME colour
      as the popup body, so the step §6 describes does not actually appear there
      and the check is the only mark. README is the contract and README asks for
      the step, so the step is what ships.
    -->
    <color name="select_item_selected">#0F000000</color>
</resources>
